1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,080
Stress incontinence — have you ever leaked a little urine when you cough, sneeze, or laugh?

2
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:12,880
It's common after childbirth and in women past midlife, but most people never talk about it.

3
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:22,240
When the pelvic floor and urethral sphincter weaken, they can't hold back the sudden pressure from a cough or a laugh, and urine leaks out.

4
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:32,160
Three everyday habits make that pressure worse: carrying extra weight, drinking a lot of caffeine, and skipping pelvic floor exercise.

5
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:34,960
First is carrying extra weight.

6
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:41,840
Every extra pound adds pressure on the bladder, so even a small cough is enough to cause a leak.

7
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:46,960
Second is drinking a lot of coffee or other caffeinated drinks.

8
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:55,120
Caffeine increases urine output and irritates the bladder, adding to the load your pelvic floor has to hold back.

9
00:00:55,440 --> 00:00:58,960
And last is skipping pelvic floor exercise.

10
00:00:59,280 --> 00:01:07,200
Like any other muscle, it weakens without regular use, so its ability to hold back pressure keeps declining.

11
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:16,800
Losing just 8 percent of body weight cuts weekly leaks nearly in half, so start there and swap caffeinated drinks for water.

12
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:28,160
Add Kegels — a 3 to 5 second squeeze and release, 10 reps, three times a day — and most women notice a real difference within 1 to 3 months.
